+ * Legacy NV volume layout (feature, not diagnostics).
+ *
+ * The NV3x era demos (Vulcan) save compressed volumes in their own format (".t3d": 12-byte
+ * header + DXT5 blocks) using DEPTH-MAJOR order: for every block column (y,x) all the blocks
+ * along depth follow in sequence. OpenGL wants SLICE-MAJOR order (z outermost). The app loads
+ * the file and passes the buffer verbatim, so on a conformant driver the volume comes out as a
+ * regular comb: in Vulcan's focus we saw horizontal stripes with a period of about 6-8 pixels.
+ *
+ * Measurement on firecomp.t3d (256^3 texels, DXT5, 1048576 blocks of 16 bytes), comparing the
+ * average difference between block descriptors at a fixed distance in the file (in blocks):
+ *
+ *    distance 1     about  8.6      distance 256   about  5.2
+ *    distance 4     about  5.1      distance 1024  about 14.3
+ *    distance 64    about 27.8      distance 4096  about 29.9
+ *    distance 16384 about 17        distance 262144 about 59
+ *
+ * A volume written as OpenGL wants (slice-major) would have row pitch 64 and slice pitch 4096,
+ * i.e. the two WORST distances; here they are the best. The file is instead a strip with row
+ * pitch 256 blocks and inner groups of 4 in depth: x has step nby*4, y has step 4, depth is
+ * split into (z%4) with step 1 and (z/4) with step nbx*nby*4.
